WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.100
People with severe loss in both balance organs can see the world bounce as they

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.300
walk. How does a healthy balance nerve prevent that?

3
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:16.573
Canals sense rotation; otoliths sense linear acceleration and tilt.

4
00:00:17.473 --> 00:00:20.633
Each organ sends both regular and irregular fibres.

5
00:00:21.817 --> 00:00:25.157
From here on, we follow canal fibres and head rotation.

6
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.490
With the head still, both fire fast.

7
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.190
A regular fibre ticks like a clock.

8
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.490
An irregular one fires with uneven gaps.

9
00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:36.993
Now a slow head turn.

10
00:00:37.873 --> 00:00:42.673
The regular fibre's rate rises and falls with head velocity, how fast the head is

11
00:00:42.673 --> 00:00:43.673
moving.

12
00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:45.357
Now a sudden change.

13
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:50.857
The irregular fibre, with its higher gain, responds more strongly, and its spikes

14
00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:51.857
lock to the event.

15
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.810
Yet regular fibres detect small motions well.

16
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.210
How can the lower-gain fibre win?

17
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.420
Pause and predict.

18
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.100
One made-up fibre has three times the gain, but ten times the CV.

19
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.020
Which detects a small, slow turn first?

20
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.550
A change in rate counts only if it stands out from the noise.

21
00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.330
For our made-up counting decoder, threshold is noise divided by gain.

22
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.323
The quiet, low-gain fibre wins.

23
00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:27.603
Its threshold comes out lower.

24
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:33.167
If our counting decoder must report within a tenth of a second, both thresholds

25
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:35.947
rise, and the regular fibre's lead shrinks.

26
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.370
So what is the irregular fibre good for?

27
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.670
Replay the same jittery head motion many times.

28
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.800
Pause and predict.

29
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.880
If irregular spikes were just noise, could their timing tell one motion from

30
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:50.880
another?

31
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.540
Their precisely timed spikes told different motions apart.

32
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.620
Irregular at rest, precise when it matters.

33
00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:06.283
Neither channel keeps reporting a steady spin.

34
00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:10.823
In a long turn at constant speed, the canal signal fades in both.

35
00:02:11.817 --> 00:02:13.377
So here is the one idea.

36
00:02:14.277 --> 00:02:18.477
The nerve reports head motion on two overlapping channels, whoever caused it.

37
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.300
In the canals, regular fibres follow how fast the head is turning, in a steady,

38
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:25.460
precise rate.

39
00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:31.613
Irregular fibres add higher gain, more response to how quickly the turn changes, and

40
00:02:31.613 --> 00:02:32.613
well-timed spikes.

41
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:38.653
In the head-posture neurons tested, your own turn is largely cancelled, when neck

42
00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:40.073
feedback matches the prediction.

43
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:44.763
Fast, precise reports are thought to help hold the world still.

44
00:02:45.703 --> 00:02:50.643
Muting matched, self-made turns in the tested neurons may help the head reflex spare

45
00:02:50.643 --> 00:02:51.623
the turns you intend.
